$DIR/main.p:10:3: the task started here takes p, a view of y, whose block ends before s waits for the task
    y is declared in the block at main.p:7, and its storage ends with that
    block. The task may run until s waits for it, after the block ends, so p
    would look at storage that no longer holds y. A copy of a view still looks
    at the same memory.
    Go keeps the variables a goroutine uses alive with its garbage collector,
    however long the goroutine runs. Plutonium has none: a variable lives in its
    function's frame until its block ends. A sync.Scope waits for the tasks it
    started before its block ends, so they may use the function's variables, and
    the compiler checks that nothing they use ends or moves first.
    Declare y in the block that declares s, or wait for the task before the
    block that declares y ends:
        s.Wait() // before the end of the block that declares y
    (see "Scoped tasks" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.4)
    (run "pluto explain E3401" for a longer explanation)
